built.changed:
Fixed the HUD's fixed-width layout that overflowed at narrow viewports (360px: horizontal scrollbar, Next panel cut off screen-right).

1. Added `<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1, viewport-fit=cover">` — the document had no viewport meta tag at all.
2. Added `html, body { max-width: 100%; overflow-x: hidden }` and `.shell { max-width: 100% }` as a horizontal-overflow guard.
3. Made `.hud` and `#field` wrap (`flex-wrap: wrap`) so they can reflow instead of forcing a fixed minimum width.
4. Added a `@media (max-width: 480px)` block that:
   - shrinks shell/body padding, heading size, and stat-box padding/font-size, and lets the 4 HUD stats flex-shrink/wrap;
   - switches `#field` from a fixed three-column row to `flex-direction: column`, so Hold, the board, and Next stack vertically (using existing DOM order — no reordering, no markup restructuring);
   - scales the Hold/Next panel canvases down to 84px wide (`height: auto`, preserving intrinsic aspect ratio) and the board canvas to `min(70vw, 200px)` wide;
   - shrinks the controls legend's max-width and font size so it wraps within the narrower shell.

Verified with headless Chrome screenshots at 360x900 (stacked: HUD row, Hold, board, Next, all within 360px width, no horizontal scrollbar) and at 800x700 (original three-column Hold/board/Next row layout is unchanged). No JS was touched — the game reads no canvas client-size/bounding-rect (keyboard-only controls), so CSS-scaling the canvases display size is safe.
